title: Windows Service Accounts
agents: windows
catalog: os/services
license: GPLv3
distribution: chexma
description:
 This check shows which Windows services do not run under a built-in system
 account but under a service account: a domain account, a local user or a
 managed service account. It needs the agent plugin {service_accounts.ps1},
 which is deployed with the agent bakery rule {Windows Service Accounts}.

 Every account gets a kind, by its SID if the agent could translate the
 account, otherwise by its name: Local System, Local Service, Network
 Service, virtual account ({NT SERVICE\...}), local user, managed service
 account (name ends with {$}), domain account or unknown. Services without
 an account, e.g. per-user service instances, are a kind of their own. By
 default Local System, Local Service, Network Service, virtual accounts and
 services without an account are built-in and not reported.

 By default the service is {WARN} for every service whose account is not on
 the allow list. With the rule {Windows Service Accounts} for the service
 you can define the allow list (which account may run which services) and
 the state for everything that is not on it, set which kinds are built-in
 accounts, ignore disabled services, define unwanted accounts with a state,
 and report changes since the service discovery (added services, changed
 accounts and removed services; services without an account are left out
 of this comparison). To accept a change, update the service parameters in
 the service discovery of the host. If the agent plugin reports an error
 the service is {UNKNOWN} (configurable).

 Patterns are regular expressions that have to match the whole account
 name or SID, ignoring case. {$HOSTNAME$} is replaced by the computer name.

 Limitations: computer accounts ({DOMAIN\HOST$}) are shown as managed
 service accounts. An account in UPN notation that cannot be translated to
 a SID is shown as domain account.

 The services under service accounts are also available in the HW/SW
 inventory under {Software > Operating system > Windows Service Accounts}.
 A host on which every service runs under a built-in account has no such
 table. The HW/SW inventory rule {Windows Service Accounts} makes the
 table list every Windows service instead.

discovery:
 One service is created for each host whose agent sends the section
 {service_accounts} with at least one service or an error.
